---
id: pan-seared-chicken-livers
title: Pan-Seared Garlic-Thyme Chicken Livers
category: modular_protein

yield:
  servings: 2
  cooked_g: 320

storage:
  fridge_days: 2
  freezer_friendly: false

---

Rinse @chicken-livers{1%package} briefly in cold water. Trim away any connective tissue, stringy bits, or greenish bile spots, and cut larger lobes into even, bite-sized halves. Pat completely dry with paper towels: surface moisture steams the livers instead of searing them.

Toss the livers in a bowl with @onion-powder{1%tsp}, @dried-thyme{1/2%tsp}, @kosher-salt{1/2%tsp}, and @black-pepper{1/4%tsp}.

Heat a large non-stick #skillet over medium-high heat and add @unsalted-butter{1.5%tbsp}. Wait until the butter is foaming hot before the livers go in.

Lay the livers in a single layer without crowding (work in two batches if the pan is under 30 cm / 12 in). Sear undisturbed for ~sear-time{2.5%min} until a deep brown crust forms underneath.

Flip the pieces and lower the heat to medium. Add @fresh-garlic{2%clove}, finely minced, and spoon the hot butter over the livers for ~baste-time{2%min}.

Splash in 2 tbsp of @chicken-broth{30%ml} and scrape up the browned bits for ~deglaze-time{30%sec} until the liquid reduces to a glossy coating. Check the thickest piece with a #meat-thermometer: pull the pan off the heat at 74°C (165°F). The centres may still look slightly pink; uniformly grey-brown centres mean they are overcooked and will taste chalky.

Transfer to a plate and rest for ~rest-time{3%min}. Divide the livers and pan glaze equally into 2 meal prep containers (about 160 g cooked per portion).
